Short answer: **❌ NO, not always.**
Long answer (important for exams & interviews) 👇

---

## 🔴 Can you use this format safely?

```java
this.name.equals(other.name);
this.age.equals(other.age);
this.bonus.equals(other.bonus);
```

### ❌ **NOT SAFE in general**

* `String`, `Integer`, `Double` are **objects**
* Objects **can be `null`**
* Calling `.equals()` on `null` causes **NullPointerException**

---

## 💥 When does it CRASH?

```java
String name = null;
String otherName = "Ravi";

name.equals(otherName); // ❌ NullPointerException
```

Same for:

* `Integer`
* `Double`
* Any object

---

## ✅ When CAN you use this format?

You can safely use:

```java
this.name.equals(other.name);
```

### ✔ ONLY IF:

* You **guarantee** the value is **never null**
* Example:

  * Mandatory fields
  * Validated in constructor
  * Final fields

---

## 🟢 SAFE ALTERNATIVE (BEST PRACTICE)

```java
Objects.equals(this.name, other.name);
```

✔ No crash
✔ Handles `null`
✔ Interview-approved

---

## 📊 Comparison Table (Very Important)

| Format                                  | Null Safe | Recommended |
| --------------------------------------- | --------- | ----------- |
| `this.name == other.name`               | ❌         | ❌           |
| `this.name.equals(other.name)`          | ❌         | ⚠️          |
| `Objects.equals(this.name, other.name)` | ✅         | ✅           |

Same applies to:

* `String`
* `Integer`
* `Double`
* Any object

---

## 🎯 Perfect Interview Answer

> “Calling `.equals()` directly on objects can cause NullPointerException.
> `Objects.equals()` is preferred because it safely handles null values.”

---

## 🧠 One-line Rule (Remember This)

> **Never call `.equals()` on a variable unless you are 100% sure it is not null.**

---

## ✅ Best Recommendation

For real projects, exams, interviews:

```java
Objects.equals(a, b)
```

